virtio-balloon (macOS): count guest RAM once, libkrun's own view swept

macOS charges a page to the footprint once for every mapping that touched it: through the
hypervisor's map when the guest touches it, and again through libkrun's own mapping of guest
RAM once a device thread did (virtio-blk and virtio-fs reads and writes, GPU transfers, vsock
and net buffers). Both map the same VM objects, so the second charge is no memory, but
Activity Monitor shows it.

A thread remaps libkrun's view of guest RAM over itself (mach_vm_remap, copy=FALSE,
VM_FLAGS_FIXED | VM_FLAGS_OVERWRITE), one 128 MB chunk at a time, 20 ms apart: the same
objects at the same offsets, without libkrun's page table entries. A pass a minute after the
balloon starts, then every 5 minutes, skipped while the footprint grew less than 64 MB since
the last one, twice in a row at most.

Device threads touch guest RAM with no lock. XNU deletes and enters the chunk's entries under
the map's write lock, so a thread that touches the chunk meanwhile faults, waits and finds the
same page. A chunk is remapped only when every entry is true shared, has an object, is
readable and writable and not wired; give_back holds a lock the sweep takes per chunk; after
each remap the entries are read again, and other objects, offsets or a hole are mapped back
from a spare mapping made before the remap and stop the sweep for the run (libkrun aborts if
that fails too). Precondition: nothing kernel-wires guest RAM (disks are files, not
/dev/rdisk devices).

Design from OmacVM's QEMU patch (qemu-hvf-own-view-sweep.patch, MIT), without its trial on
scratch memory.
Applied by nixos-gpu-vm's build.sh after 9901.

